Florida family believes an officer who  hit and killed their loved one should  have lost his badge before the deadly  crash 62 year-old Blaine land was  crossing the street in May when Officer  Timothy James crashed into him land died  from his injuries  officer James was responding to a  robbery call the incident was ruled an  accident  when lands family and their attorney  began investigating this crash they  found the officer had nearly a dozen  complaints against him  Omar Villafranca this morning looks at  their search for justice not having the  answers it absolutely does delay your  grieving Stacy land was heartbroken  after her brother Blaine was struck and  killed in May by an on-duty Jacksonville  sheriff's officer responding to a call  officer Timothy L James was immediately  put on administrative leave but never  arrested one month later the same  officer was arrested for allegedly  beating a handcuffed teenager  James has pleaded not guilty to the  beating he has been reassigned to desk  duty and stripped of his law enforcement  powers but the alleged beating may not  be an isolated incident a Jacksonville  Sheriff's Office document shows that 11  complaints have been filed against  officer James since 2014 charges include  violating firearms policy and lying to  supervisors James received a 10-day  suspension for the latter in April cell  phone footage surfaced of what appears  to be officer Tim James spitting on a  man outside a hospital James has not  responded to our request for an  interview the Jacksonville Sheriff's  Department says Florida law protects  officers privacy we cannot identify an  officer under investigation  but Stacy land wanted to know more her  lawyer put in a public records request  for more information on officer James  and the department's history the  sheriff's office sent an invoice  totaling three hundred and fourteen  thousand six hundred eighty seven  dollars and 91 cents it was ridiculous  it's clearly meant to dissuade us from  looking further the sheriff's office  said the request was broad and included  all of the materials in a sixteen  hundred cubic foot room  also like to correct against some of  that narrative the South air that we  have you know been obstructionist and  charts 300 grand for one officers record  when that's just not completely accurate  officer James is just one of seven  Jacksonville officers who have been  arrested since January charges against  the other officers include tampering  with evidence and aggravated assault  prosecutors have dropped charges in 41  cases that rely on testimony of recently  arrested officers as for Stacy land she  says she wants justice for her brother  my brother's debt to me is is the  perfect storm out of a broken department  with messed up processes and people  looking the other way and an officer who  should not be on the street  for CBS this morning Omar Villafranca
 

